./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/TASK-260715-2hiabd_implement-ios-packet-tunnel-provider-adapter/README.md:4:Implement the iOS NEPacketTunnelProvider target adapter that translates Apple lifecycle calls and packetFlow access into the shared production factories and TunnelRuntimeCoordinator. Keep policy and data-plane state in ReluxTunnelCore and use only public APIs.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/TASK-260715-2hiabd_implement-ios-packet-tunnel-provider-adapter/README.md:10:1. The adapter contains only platform translation and composition and has no independent packet, SSH, TCP, DNS, or route state machine. 2. startTunnel completes only when the shared runtime reaches its M1 usable point or returns one mapped error exactly once. 3. stopTunnel cancels the active generation, waits for bounded cleanup, records the Apple stop reason, and calls its completion exactly once. 4. App messages are size-bounded and delegated to the shared versioned router even after the containing app has relaunched. 5. The iOS provider builds with approved public APIs and automated adapter tests cover duplicate start, stop during start, mapped failure, late callback, and deallocation.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/TASK-260715-3dv8ea_implement-macos-packet-tunnel-provider-adapter/README.md:4:Implement the macOS NEPacketTunnelProvider target adapter that translates Apple lifecycle calls and packetFlow access into the same shared production factories and TunnelRuntimeCoordinator used on iOS, while preserving the generated product boundary from the legacy app.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-1y04r0_shared-tunnel-runtime/TASK-260715-3ejhyy_compose-m0-runtime-dependencies/README.md:10:1. The macOS production root and deterministic SPM harness root satisfy the same candidate-neutral session interfaces, while only production requires a valid accepted binding manifest. 2. Each generation creates packet, HEV, libssh2 SSH, TCP and DNS objects at most once; prepare acquires no descriptor, HEV lease/thread, private listener or PacketFlow read; activateReads separately starts the accepted bridge after settings; stop releases every acquired object in contract order. 3. Production fails before routes when composition is not permitted, any accepted resource digest or pin differs, a binding is superseded, or a mandatory capability is absent; no local default or task note substitutes. 4. No libssh2 concrete type, HEV C API, NEPacketTunnelProvider implementation, host-app object or live platform callback crosses an unintended shared boundary. 5. macOS and harness compile/smoke tests prove exact bound component resolution, pure preflight, post-settings start, DNS-only UDP dispatch, deterministic substitution, repeated cleanup and boundary-guard rejection of type leakage.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-1y04r0_shared-tunnel-runtime/BUG-260728-3jfjkh_stabilize-provider-failure-first-callback-ordering/README.md:4:A reproducible race in TunnelProviderAdapter.providerDidFail lets two sequential provider-failure callbacks be admitted by unordered Tasks; the second error may win, so ProviderAdapterContractTests.providerFailureHandoff intermittently returns internalInvariant 1009 instead of the first runtimeStartupFailed 1007. Diagnose and restore deterministic exactly-once first-call semantics without weakening lifecycle cleanup.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-1y04r0_shared-tunnel-runtime/BUG-260728-3jfjkh_stabilize-provider-failure-first-callback-ordering/README.md:10:1. Two sequential providerDidFail calls deterministically deliver the first call error exactly once on both adapter seams. 2. Concurrent/reentrant calls remain exactly-once and do not deadlock or leak cleanup. 3. The focused providerFailureHandoff test passes at least 30 consecutive runs. 4. The complete swift test suite passes with real exit 0. 5. The fix is minimal, preserves provider-failure stop/join semantics, and is independently reviewed.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/TASK-260715-1q4qhw_record-vpn-configuration-lifecycle-contract/README.md:4:Produce the task-scoped contract for how both containing apps own one NETunnelProviderManager configuration and how both NEPacketTunnelProvider adapters delegate to the shared runtime. Define identity, persistence, permission, command, status, app-message, error, stop, and cleanup behavior before platform implementation.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/TASK-260715-1bp6eu_implement-provider-message-router-and-stop-cleanup/README.md:10:1. Only getProtocolCapabilities, getRuntimeSnapshot, getCapabilities, and getDiagnostics are accepted in v1; every non-nil handler receives exactly one bounded response/error and no message can expose or mutate secrets/configuration. 2. Malformed, oversized, duplicate-key, unknown/future, duplicate, concurrent, nil-handler, and retired-generation inputs cannot crash, block, or update another generation. 3. Stop rejects new work, cancels start/runtime, joins accepted reverse cleanup, and completes every registered Apple stop gate once within the 10-second policy; expiry force-closes controllable handles and records a redacted deadline failure. 4. Stop during start completes the pending start gate with cancellation before stop gates; provider fatal failure calls cancelTunnelWithError once and the later system stop joins existing cleanup. 5. Shared tests run through both adapter seams and cover every Apple stop reason, every startup cancellation point, deadline, duplicate/late callbacks, route-clear uncertainty, deallocation, and 100-cycle baselines.
./.task-board/EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260717-1ecq74_macos-self-update/TASK-260717-2uyfn5_select-and-record-macos-self-update-mechanism/README.md:10:1. A dated research report cites official Sparkle and Apple primary sources for the selected exact Sparkle 2 baseline, license, EdDSA/appcast requirements, helper/sandbox/hardened-runtime behavior, and update installation lifecycle; volatile facts are verified rather than recalled. 2. ADR-018 and the self-update spec record the exact framework/version policy, dependency integration shape, stable/prerelease channel/feed contract, and the invariant that every payload is an already-notarized Developer ID build before appcast publication. 3. The decision defines fail-closed signature/feed behavior, rollback/downgrade/withdrawal, key generation/custody/rotation/revocation boundaries, privacy/telemetry rules, and the exact human release gates without storing or generating any secret. 4. Network Extension replacement, host relaunch, system approval/reapproval uncertainty, helper/XPC, sandbox, and hardened-runtime claims are bounded to what primary evidence proves; physical clean-install/update validation remains an explicit later gate. 5. A TASK-ID-scoped outcome maps evidence to downstream TASK-260717-xempiv and TASK-260717-1mt4e7, records validation/privacy checks and residual gates, and a fresh independent Codex reviewer accepts the decision before done.
